    "description": "Original MCQs for a German antitrust/competition-law (Kartellrecht) compliance-training module, covering the cartel prohibition and the market-dominance test (Sec. 1/18 GWB, mirroring Art. 101 TFEU; Sec. 19 GWB's specific abuse-of-dominance categories and Art. 102 TFEU are researched but not yet covered by a dedicated question in this pilot round - flagged as a gap for the next scale-up), hardcore restrictions and real-world risk scenarios (trade-association information exchange, no-poach agreements, non-price cartels), the fine framework and the role of compliance programs in fine mitigation (Sec. 81c/81d GWB), self-cleaning for regaining public-tender eligibility (Sec. 125 GWB), and individual criminal liability for bid-rigging (Sec. 298 StGB) - including three real, named 2025-2026 enforcement examples (German road-construction bid-rigging, EU automotive-recycling cartel, Delivery Hero/Glovo no-poach case). Content is independently phrased and grounded directly in GWB/StGB statute text and primary enforcement sources (Bundeskartellamt press releases, EU Commission press releases), cross-checked via dejure.org and named case sources, NOT sourced or paraphrased from any commercial compliance-training vendor's catalog. This is a fully independent exam module - separate content pool, separate exam simulation from every other module. Confirmed: unlike the GwG (money-laundering law), there is no statutorily fixed training-frequency mandate for antitrust compliance training.",
